---
import LearnArticle from "../../components/LearnArticle.astro";
import DemoFrame from "../../components/DemoFrame.astro";
import RelatedLink from "../../components/RelatedLink.astro";
---

<LearnArticle
	href="/learn/public-key-cryptography"
	description="Public-key cryptography explained with open padlocks: why a public key can lock but only a private key can unlock, with a step-by-step animation of a message's journey."
	lead="How can two strangers on opposite sides of the world share a secret — when everything they send could be seen by anyone?"
>
	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">The Key-Sharing Problem</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			On the <a href="/learn/encryption-basics">Encryption Basics</a> page, the same key locked and
			unlocked the message. That works — but first you have to get the key to the other person. If you
			send it over the internet, anyone watching could copy it, and then read everything you send
			afterwards.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			You can't meet in person with every website you use. So how does your browser set up a secret with
			a website it has never talked to before?
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">The Open Padlock Trick</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Imagine Sam buys a box of padlocks that all open with one key. Sam keeps the key safe at home, and
			hands out <em>open</em> padlocks to anyone who asks — friends, strangers, it doesn't matter.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Anyone can snap one of Sam's padlocks shut on a box. But once it's closed, only Sam's key can open
			it. Not even the person who locked it can get back in.
		</p>
		<div class="compare-grid">
			<div class="compare-card">
				<p class="compare-card-title">Public key = the open padlock</p>
				<p>Shared with everyone. Anyone can use it to <em>lock</em> a message for Sam.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="compare-card compare-card--accent">
				<p class="compare-card-title">Private key = the only key</p>
				<p>Never shared. It's the only thing that can <em>unlock</em> those messages.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
		<p class="block-callout">
			Two different keys: one for locking that everyone can have, one for unlocking that only the owner
			has. That's why it's also called <em>asymmetric</em> encryption.
		</p>
	</section>

	<DemoFrame label="Public-key cryptography animation" title="Watch a message travel">
		<div class="pk-demo" data-pk-demo>
			<div class="demo-field">
				<label for="pk-message">Alex's message</label>
				<input id="pk-message" class="demo-input pk-message" type="text" maxlength="32" value="Meet at the library at 4" autocomplete="off" data-pk-message />
			</div>

			<div class="pk-stage" data-pk-stage data-step="0" aria-hidden="true">
				<div class="pk-person pk-person--alex">
					<span class="pk-avatar">A</span>
					<span class="pk-name">Alex</span>
					<span class="pk-slot pk-alex-lock">
						<svg viewBox="0 0 32 40" class="pk-icon pk-icon--lock-open"><path d="M9 18V11a7 7 0 0 1 14 0" /><rect x="5" y="18" width="22" height="18" rx="3" /></svg>
					</span>
				</div>
				<div class="pk-person pk-person--eve">
					<span class="pk-avatar">E</span>
					<span class="pk-name">Eve, listening in</span>
					<span class="pk-slot pk-eve-lock">
						<svg viewBox="0 0 32 40" class="pk-icon pk-icon--lock-open"><path d="M9 18V11a7 7 0 0 1 14 0" /><rect x="5" y="18" width="22" height="18" rx="3" /></svg>
					</span>
					<span class="pk-eve-fail">✕ Can't open it</span>
				</div>
				<div class="pk-person pk-person--sam">
					<span class="pk-avatar">S</span>
					<span class="pk-name">Sam</span>
					<span class="pk-slot pk-sam-keys">
						<svg viewBox="0 0 40 24" class="pk-icon pk-icon--key"><circle cx="9" cy="12" r="6" /><path d="M15 12h21M30 12v6M35 12v5" /></svg>
						<svg viewBox="0 0 32 40" class="pk-icon pk-icon--lock-open"><path d="M9 18V11a7 7 0 0 1 14 0" /><rect x="5" y="18" width="22" height="18" rx="3" /></svg>
					</span>
				</div>

				<div class="pk-wire"></div>

				<div class="pk-box" data-pk-box>
					<svg viewBox="0 0 32 40" class="pk-icon pk-box-lock pk-box-lock--closed"><path d="M9 18V11a7 7 0 0 1 14 0v7" /><rect x="5" y="18" width="22" height="18" rx="3" /></svg>
					<span class="pk-box-content" data-pk-box-content></span>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p class="pk-step-count" data-pk-count>Step 1 of 6</p>
			<p class="demo-status pk-caption" data-pk-caption aria-live="polite"></p>

			<div class="demo-controls">
				<button type="button" class="demo-btn" data-pk-back>← Back</button>
				<button type="button" class="demo-btn demo-btn--primary" data-pk-next>Next →</button>
				<button type="button" class="demo-btn" data-pk-restart>Start over</button>
			</div>
			<p class="demo-note">The scrambled text is an illustration — real encrypted data is just as unreadable, but made with far more complex math.</p>
		</div>
	</DemoFrame>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">Why Can't Eve Just Work Out the Key?</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			The public and private keys are linked by math that is easy to do in one direction and practically
			impossible to undo. Here's a small taste:
		</p>
		<ul class="block-list">
			<li>Multiplying is easy: <span class="mono-highlight">61 × 53 = 3,233</span>.</li>
			<li>
				Going backwards is much harder: "which two numbers multiply to make 3,233?" means trying lots of
				possibilities.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p class="block-text">
			Real keys use numbers hundreds of digits long. Multiplying them takes a computer a split second.
			Splitting the answer back into the original numbers would take every computer on Earth longer
			than a human lifetime — many lifetimes. That one-way difficulty is what keeps the private key safe
			even though the public key is out in the open.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">You Use This Every Day</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			When you see the padlock next to a website's address, public-key encryption has just been used.
			Your browser used the website's public key to safely agree on a brand-new shared secret key. From
			then on, both sides switch to ordinary, faster same-key encryption using that secret. The public-key
			step solved the "how do we share a key?" problem; the same-key step does the heavy lifting.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">But Is It Really Sam's Padlock?</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			There's one more trick an attacker could try: handing out their <em>own</em> padlocks while
			pretending to be Sam. To stop this, websites get their public keys vouched for by a trusted
			organization called a <em>certificate authority</em> — a bit like an official ID card that says
			"this padlock really does belong to this bank." Your browser checks that ID automatically every
			time.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			The same pair of keys can also work the other way round. If Sam locks something with the
			<em>private</em> key, anyone can open it with the public key — which proves it could only have come
			from Sam. That's called a <em>digital signature</em>.
		</p>
		<RelatedLink href="/learn/encryption-basics" label="Back to basics" />
	</section>
</LearnArticle>

<script>
	const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-pk-demo]")!;
	const stage = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-pk-stage]")!;
	const input = root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("[data-pk-message]")!;
	const boxContent = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-pk-box-content]")!;
	const caption = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-pk-caption]")!;
	const countEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-pk-count]")!;
	const backBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-pk-back]")!;
	const nextBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-pk-next]")!;
	const restartBtn = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-pk-restart]")!;

	const STEPS = [
		"Sam makes a matching pair: open padlocks (the public key) and the only key that opens them (the private key). The key never leaves Sam.",
		"Sam hands out open padlocks to anyone — including Alex, and Eve, who is secretly watching. That's fine: a padlock can only lock things.",
		"Alex writes a message and puts it in a box.",
		"Alex snaps Sam's padlock shut. The message is now scrambled — and even Alex can't open the box again.",
		"The box travels across the internet. Eve grabs a copy, but Eve only has a padlock, not the key. All Eve sees is nonsense.",
		"The box reaches Sam. Sam's private key opens the padlock, and the message is readable again.",
	];
	let step = 0;

	// A stable, random-looking scramble of the message, shown as hex bytes.
	// Purely illustrative — see the note under the demo.
	function scramble(text: string): string {
		let seed = 0x9e3779b9;
		const bytes = Array.from(new TextEncoder().encode(text)).map((b) => {
			seed ^= seed << 13;
			seed ^= seed >>> 17;
			seed ^= seed << 5;
			return (b ^ seed) & 0xff;
		});
		return bytes
			.slice(0, 12)
			.map((b) => b.toString(16).toUpperCase().padStart(2, "0"))
			.join(" ");
	}

	function render() {
		stage.dataset.step = String(step);
		const message = input.value.trim() || "(empty message)";
		if (step < 2) boxContent.textContent = "";
		else if (step === 2 || step === 5) boxContent.textContent = message;
		else boxContent.textContent = scramble(message);
		boxContent.classList.toggle("is-scrambled", step === 3 || step === 4);
		caption.textContent = STEPS[step];
		countEl.textContent = `Step ${step + 1} of ${STEPS.length}`;
		backBtn.disabled = step === 0;
		nextBtn.disabled = step === STEPS.length - 1;
	}

	backBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
		step = Math.max(0, step - 1);
		render();
	});
	nextBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
		step = Math.min(STEPS.length - 1, step + 1);
		render();
	});
	restartBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
		step = 0;
		render();
	});
	input.addEventListener("input", render);

	render();
</script>

<style is:global>
	.pk-demo .pk-message {
		width: min(100%, 20em);
	}

	/* Three people across a "wire"; the box and padlock copies appear and
	   move between them according to data-step (0–5). Positions are in %
	   of the stage so the scene scales with the frame. */
	.pk-demo .pk-stage {
		position: relative;
		height: 17em;
		margin: 1em auto 0;
		max-width: 46em;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-person {
		position: absolute;
		top: 0;
		width: 30%;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.25em;
		text-align: center;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-person--alex {
		left: 0;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-person--eve {
		left: 35%;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-person--sam {
		right: 0;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-avatar {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		width: 2.4em;
		height: 2.4em;
		border-radius: 50%;
		border: 2px solid var(--color-text-muted);
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		color: var(--color-text);
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-person--sam .pk-avatar {
		border-color: var(--color-accent);
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-person--eve .pk-avatar {
		border-style: dashed;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-name {
		font-size: 0.85em;
		font-weight: 600;
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-slot {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.3em;
		min-height: 2em;
		transition: opacity 0.4s ease;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-icon {
		fill: none;
		stroke-width: 3;
		stroke-linecap: round;
		stroke-linejoin: round;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-icon--lock-open {
		width: 1.4em;
		height: 1.75em;
		stroke: #fff3d6;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-icon--key {
		width: 2.2em;
		height: 1.3em;
		stroke: var(--color-accent);
	}

	/* Padlock copies only exist once Sam has handed them out (step 1+). */
	.pk-demo .pk-alex-lock,
	.pk-demo .pk-eve-lock {
		opacity: 0;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-stage:not([data-step="0"]) .pk-alex-lock,
	.pk-demo .pk-stage:not([data-step="0"]) .pk-eve-lock {
		opacity: 1;
	}

	/* Alex's padlock is used up once it's snapped onto the box. */
	.pk-demo .pk-stage[data-step="3"] .pk-alex-lock,
	.pk-demo .pk-stage[data-step="4"] .pk-alex-lock,
	.pk-demo .pk-stage[data-step="5"] .pk-alex-lock {
		opacity: 0;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-eve-fail {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 0.85em;
		white-space: nowrap;
		color: var(--color-text);
		opacity: 0;
		transition: opacity 0.3s ease 0.5s;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-stage[data-step="4"] .pk-eve-fail {
		opacity: 1;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-wire {
		position: absolute;
		left: 12%;
		right: 12%;
		top: 12.8em;
		height: 0;
		border-top: 2px dashed var(--color-border);
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-box {
		position: absolute;
		top: 10.5em;
		left: 15%;
		width: 9.5em;
		min-height: 4.6em;
		margin-left: -4.75em;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		gap: 0.2em;
		padding: 0.4em;
		border-radius: 8px;
		border: 2px solid var(--color-text-muted);
		background: var(--color-surface-alt);
		opacity: 0;
		transition:
			left 0.9s ease,
			opacity 0.4s ease,
			border-color 0.3s ease;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-stage:is([data-step="2"], [data-step="3"], [data-step="4"], [data-step="5"]) .pk-box {
		opacity: 1;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-stage[data-step="4"] .pk-box {
		left: 50%;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-stage[data-step="5"] .pk-box {
		left: 85%;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-stage:is([data-step="3"], [data-step="4"]) .pk-box {
		border-color: var(--color-accent);
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-box-lock {
		width: 1.3em;
		height: 1.6em;
		stroke: var(--color-accent);
		display: none;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-stage:is([data-step="3"], [data-step="4"]) .pk-box-lock {
		display: block;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-box-content {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 0.78em;
		line-height: 1.3;
		text-align: center;
		color: var(--color-text);
		overflow-wrap: anywhere;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-box-content.is-scrambled {
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		letter-spacing: 0.04em;
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-step-count {
		margin: 0.8em 0 0;
		text-align: center;
		font-size: 0.78em;
		font-weight: 700;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
		color: var(--color-accent);
	}

	.pk-demo .pk-caption {
		margin-top: 0.3em;
		min-height: 3em;
	}

	@media (max-width: 480px) {
		.pk-demo .pk-name {
			font-size: 0.75em;
		}

		.pk-demo .pk-box {
			width: 7.5em;
			margin-left: -3.75em;
		}

		.pk-demo .pk-stage[data-step="2"] .pk-box,
		.pk-demo .pk-stage[data-step="3"] .pk-box {
			left: 20%;
		}

		.pk-demo .pk-stage[data-step="5"] .pk-box {
			left: 80%;
		}
	}
</style>
